<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ethiopian–Adal War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1527">1527</a>–<a href="/years/1543">1543</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Ethiopian-Adal War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ethiopian–Adal War was a conflict fought in 1527–1543 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi</a>, and the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>. Adal forces overran most of the highlands, but Portuguese intervention and Ahmad's death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wayna-daga">Battle of Wayna Daga</a> in 1543 ended the conquest, leaving both states exhausted.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ethiopian-adal-war.jpg" alt="Ethiopian–Adal War" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Horniman Museum, London, UK. Photographic Rights The Bridgeman Art Library · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deaths_of_Gragn_and_Da_Gama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Early in the 16th century the Adalites under Imam Mahfuz had pressed on Ethiopian territory. His death at the hands of the Ethiopians under Lebna Dengel was followed by a long succession crisis in Adal, the Adalite Civil War, which ended with Ahmad in control and the advocates of peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in a minority. The Futuh al-Habasha, the main Arabic chronicle of the war, gives no reason for the invasion. Modern historians point to resentment at paying tribute to a non-Muslim state, the wealth of the highlands and the messianic hopes around Ahmad, whom his chronicler calls Imam of the End of Time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Christian sources record a raid into Muslim border territory in 1527, and the Futuh describes clashes at Badeqe in September 1528 and at Shimbra Kure in March 1529, where Ahmad's forces beat a larger Ethiopian army. The decisive victories came in 1531, at Antukyah on 23 February, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> fire panicked the Ethiopian soldiers, and at Ayfars on 31 March. Lebna Dengel narrowly escaped capture at Amba Sel and spent his last years moving through his realm until he died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-damo">Debre Damo</a> on 2 September 1540.</p><p>The Adal forces burned churches across the highlands, among them Atronsa Maryam, where several emperors were buried, and at Aksum Ahmad destroyed the Church of Our Lady Mary of Zion; his chronicler put the Christian dead in Tigray at over 10,000. In February 1541 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cristovao-da-gama">Cristóvão da Gama</a> landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a> with 400 Portuguese musketeers. Ahmad, wounded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalo">Antalo</a>, obtained ten field guns and 700 soldiers from the Ottoman forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a>, and on 28 August 1542 da Gama was captured and killed on his order.</p><p>The surviving Portuguese joined Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galawdewos">Gelawdewos</a>, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wayna-daga">Battle of Wayna Daga</a> near Lake Tana Ahmad was shot and then beheaded on 21 February 1543. His soldiers fled when they learned of his death, and the Adal army withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In October 1544 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galawdewos">Gelawdewos</a> defeated and killed the vizier Abbas, who had tried to hold the border provinces, and recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, Fatagar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawaro">Dawaro</a> and Hadiya. He gave lands to about 170 of the Portuguese, but relations soured over attempts to impose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, and he answered the friars with his own Confession of Faith.</p><p>The war brought heavy losses to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harari">Harari</a> people and is counted among the causes of their decline. Mohammed Hassen has argued that by weakening both sides it opened the way for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> to move into Welega and up to the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a>. Many historians trace hostile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> relations to the war, which was soon followed by the Ottoman–Ethiopian War.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ethiopian–Adal War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Adal Sultanate's historical impact was felt most directly through the <b>Ethiopian–Adal War</b> of 1529–1543, one of the most consequential conflicts in the history of the Horn of Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
